      <article id="identify-files" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Inspection</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Easily identify files</h2>
        <div class="index-prose mb-3">
          <p>
            In the DOS and Amiga era it was common for groups to use false filename extensions to either brand files with their acronyms or to obfuscate the purpose of the file.
            Linux and macOS include the great, often forgotten <code>file</code> terminal tool for determining file types.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-dark text-light rounded-2 p-3 overflow-x-auto">
<pre class="font-monospace small mb-0">$ file ART.GRP
  ART.GRP: ISO-8859 text, with CRLF line terminators,
  with escape sequences

$ file VAL.DAT
  VAL.DAT: LHa (2.x) archive data

$ file image.iff
  image.iff: IFF data, ILBM interleaved image, 320 x 54</pre>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="deark" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Format analysis</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Obscure files with Deark</h2>
        <div class="index-prose mb-3">
          <p>
            While the <code>file</code> tool is great, it is not always useful for ancient file content created on the microcomputers or PCs of the 1980s.
            For this, the best tool is <a href="https://github.com/jsummers/deark" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deark</a>, <q>a utility for file format and metadata analysis</q>.
          </p>
          <p>
            This example is an image created in 1984's <a href="https://winworldpc.com/product/pc-paint/100a" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PCPaint</a>.
            Modern tools cannot recognize it, while Deark identifies the picture and converts it to a PNG image, <a href="/image/new/output.000.png">output.000.png</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-dark text-light rounded-2 p-3 overflow-x-auto">
<pre class="font-monospace small mb-0">$ file VIEW2KIL.PIC
  VIEW2KIL.PIC: data

$ deark VIEW2KIL.PIC
  Module: bsave
  Format: BSAVE-PC 4-color, 320x200
  Warning: BSAVE formats can't be reliably identified.
  Writing output.000.png</pre>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="vscodium" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Plain text &amp; NFOs</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Reading texts with VSCodium</h2>
        <div class="row g-3 align-items-start mb-3">
          <div class="col-md-8 index-prose">
            <p>
              <strong><a href="https://vscodium.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VSCodium</a></strong> is the free, open-source build of Microsoft's VS Code editor,
              without the bloat of Microsoft programming extensions, telemetry, or clanker AI integration.
              VSCodium supports extremely large files, offers a mini-map preview, and provides <u>the best Code Page text support</u> of any modern editor.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="col-md-4">
            <img src="/image/new/vscodium.webp" loading="lazy" class="img-fluid rounded border shadow-sm w-100" alt="VSCodium app screenshot reading a text file">
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="border-top pt-3 small">
          <div class="fw-semibold mb-2">Recommended tweaks for VSCodium:</div>
          <ol class="mb-0 text-body-secondary">
            <li>Navigate to <code>File &gt; Preferences &gt; Settings</code></li>
            <li>Search for <code>files:encoding</code>, then change <q>UTF-8</q> to <q>DOS (CP 437)</q></li>
            <li>Search for <code>editor:language detection</code>, then uncheck <code>Workbench &gt; Editor: Language Detection</code></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="ansi-art" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Color text art</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Reading ANSI text art</h2>
        <div class="row g-3 align-items-start mb-3">
          <div class="col-md-8 index-prose">
            <p>
              <strong><a href="https://blocktronics.github.io/moebius/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Moebius</a></strong> is a feature-complete ANSI art editor and a good choice to use as a viewer on modern systems.
              I do not use it for plain text or NFO text files as it isn't as performant as VSCodium and does not handle long text files well.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong><a href="https://github.com/bengarrett/retrotxtgo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">retrotxtgo</a></strong> is a terminal application that correctly prints legacy CP-437 encoded text and ANSI files in a modern terminal emulator:
            </p>
            <div class="bg-dark text-light rounded-2 p-2 px-3 overflow-x-auto mb-3">
<pre class="font-monospace small mb-0">$ retrotxt view RELEASE.NFO</pre>
            </div>
            <p>
              There is also the <a href="https://retrotxt.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RetroTxt web extension</a> that renders ANSI art and text in the browser like HTML.
              However, because browsers are very cautious with rendering, not all ANSI art will work; at times the browser treats a file as binary data and refuses to render the page.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="col-md-4">
            <img src="/image/new/moebius.webp" loading="lazy" class="img-fluid rounded border shadow-sm w-100" alt="Moebius app screenshot displaying ANSI art">
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="dos-programs" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Emulation</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Running DOS programs</h2>
        <div class="row g-3 align-items-start mb-3">
          <div class="col-md-8 index-prose">
            <p>
              DOS on the PC is the original operating system for the Intel x86 computer platform
              and was in wide use from 1981 until the late 1990s, when Microsoft's Windows took over.
              DOS lifted a number of ideas and syntax from the earlier CP/M, a system that was popular in the 1970s,
              meaning its limited parser, hardware configurations, and usage are primitive,
              which is why we don't usually bother <a href="https://86box.net" target="_blank" rel="noopener">emulating</a> full PC hardware for it.
            </p>
            <p class="small text-body-secondary">
              Different companies offered DOS on the PC: the most famous is MS-DOS by Microsoft,
              alongside IBM PC-DOS and Digital Research's DR-DOS, and they are all inter-compatible.
              Confusingly, you may also read of Apple DOS or Atari DOS, but these are unrelated to the PC.
            </p>
            <p>
              Instead, <strong><a href="https://dosbox-x.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOSBox-X</a></strong> or <strong><a href="https://www.dosbox-staging.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOSBox Staging</a></strong> are the easiest options for running DOS applications on modern systems.
              While DOSBox still has a learning curve due to the quirks of the PC platform,
              it is a great ecosystem for getting DOS software working and is generally simpler than using real hardware.
            </p>
            <p>
              The original DOSBox software hasn't been updated in years,
              but DOSBox Staging has taken the source and continued improvements.
              DOSBox-X is also an option for those who want a graphical interface for configurations,
              though its menus and dialogs can be hard to navigate.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="col-md-4">
            <img src="/image/new/dosbox.webp" loading="lazy" class="img-fluid rounded border shadow-sm w-100" alt="DOSBox app running an intro screenshot">
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="old-windows" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Virtualization</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Running old Windows programs</h2>
        <div class="index-prose mb-3">
          <p>
            Because there are multiple generations of Windows spanning decades (Windows 3.x, 9x, NT, 2000, XP, 7),
            software compatibility depends on the Windows target and the programming APIs that the software uses:
          </p>
          <ul class="mb-3">
            <li>For most software targeting the earlier Windows 3.x and Windows 9x era of the 1990s, <strong>Windows 2000</strong> is a reliable platform.</li>
            <li>For Windows software from the 2000s and some of the 2010s, <strong>Windows XP</strong> often works best, as it was the longest-supported edition of that era.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="row g-3 align-items-start mb-3">
          <div class="col-md-8 index-prose">
            <p>
              I use an old Intel Mac combined with <strong>Parallels Desktop</strong> for running both Windows 2000 and Windows XP.
              Parallels Desktop <a href="https://kb.parallels.com/129252" target="_blank" rel="noopener">up to release 18.x</a> (from 2022) offers sound, graphics, mouse, and networking for both Windows 2000 and XP,
              as well as clipboard support for copying text and files between the Mac and Windows desktops, plus saved-state snapshots.
              Parallels is much easier to set up for legacy Windows compared to the free but janky <a href="https://www.virtualbox.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VirtualBox</a> option.
            </p>
            <p>
              Used Intel Macs are cheap these days; just make sure it has at least 8 GB of memory and a solid-state drive.
              Old releases of Parallels Desktop were sold boxed at retailers (avoid the one-year subscription editions).
              Older copies of Windows can be found second-hand, at <a href="https://winworldpc.com/library/operating-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WinWorld</a>,
              or on archive.org by searching <q>windows xp service pack 3</q>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="col-md-4">
            <img src="/image/new/parallel.webp" loading="lazy" class="img-fluid rounded border shadow-sm w-100" alt="Parallels Desktop app running Windows screenshot">
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="alert alert-warning small mb-0" role="alert">
          <strong>Important:</strong> Do not connect ancient copies of Windows to the Internet.
          Instead, use the host machine to download and extract files, then copy them into your offline Windows 2000 or XP virtual machine.
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="ansimations" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Animation</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">ANSImations</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            In late 2026, Defacto2 incorporated ANSImation support into the website's ecosystem.
            These are displayed as WebP animations (similar to a more optimized animated GIF)
            and are generated using a purpose-built tool we created and you can use, <a href="https://github.com/bengarrett/ansimate" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ANSImate</a>.
            There is also a <a href="https://bengarrett.github.io/ansimate/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">web interface</a> so you can preview and convert ANSImations right in your browser.
          </p>
          <p>
            Another way to play them is inside DOSBox or one of its derivatives: slow down the CPU cycles by tapping <kbd>F12</kbd> <kbd>-</kbd>,
            then use the <code>TYPE</code> command to play the ANSI animation:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-dark text-light rounded-2 p-2 px-3 overflow-x-auto mb-3">
<pre class="font-monospace small mb-0">C:\&gt; TYPE JD-ANIM.ANS</pre>
          </div>
          <p>
            You can also run one of the many ANSI art viewers written by the artscene in the 1990s under DOSBox,
            such as <a href="https://demozoo.org/productions/270071/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TRiBEview</a> or <a href="/f/ac44e5">ACiD View</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <div id="images-videos" class="row g-4" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;">
        <div class="col-md-6">
          <article class="changelog-entry p-4 h-100">
            <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Graphics</div>
            <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Viewing old image formats</h2>
            <p class="small">
              Many image formats that were commonplace in the 16-bit microcomputer era were never adopted by the web or digital photography and have been forgotten.
              The most common incompatible format on the site is the Amiga IFF (Interchange File Format), which usually uses the <code>.iff</code> or <code>.lbm</code> filename extensions.
            </p>
            <p class="small mb-0">
              <strong><a href="https://www.xnview.com/en/xnview/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">XnView</a></strong> is the best tool for viewing these legacy formats on modern systems.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
          <article class="changelog-entry p-4 h-100">
            <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Video</div>
            <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Viewing old videos</h2>
            <p class="small mb-0">
              Most of the old AVI video codecs used by sceners for promotional and bumper videos, etc., are compatible with <strong><a href="https://www.videolan.org/vlc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VLC media player</a></strong>.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>

      <article id="bonus-tools" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Utilities</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Bonus tools</h2>

        <section class="pb-4 mb-4 border-bottom">
          <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-2">Duplicate file eraser: <code>fdupes</code> <span class="small text-body-secondary fw-normal">by Adrian Lopez</span></h3>
          <div class="index-prose mb-3">
            <p>
              The <code>fdupes</code> utility is the best duplicate finder we have used and is available on most Linux distributions as well as Homebrew for macOS.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-dark text-light rounded-2 p-3 overflow-x-auto">
<pre class="font-monospace small mb-0"># List duplicate files
fdupes --recurse --time --size .

# Interactively select and remove dupes
fdupes --recurse --order=time --delete .

# Batch delete without prompts
fdupes --recurse --order=time --delete --noprompt .</pre>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="pb-4 mb-4 border-bottom">
          <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-2">Hex editor: <a href="https://github.com/WerWolv/ImHex" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ImHex</a></h3>
          <div class="row g-3 align-items-start">
            <div class="col-md-8 index-prose">
              <p>
                There are numerous hex editors on every platform, but the open-source, multi-platform ImHex is especially useful for browsing, categorizing, and searching string data inside a file.
                It is amazing how much additional 1980s Scene history can be uncovered from embedded texts injected into binary files.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <img src="/image/new/imhex.webp" loading="lazy" class="img-fluid rounded border shadow-sm w-100" alt="ImHex app viewing a hacked DOS binary screenshot">
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-2">Old ZIP archive adverts: <a href="https://github.com/bengarrett/zipcmt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">zipcmt</a></h3>
          <div class="index-prose">
            <p>
              In the BBS era, it was very common for boards and some early websites to embed adverts into ZIP archive comments.
              The adverts were intended for display when extracted in a terminal or MS-DOS command prompt.
              Zip Comment lets you view, browse, and save any of these forgotten adverts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </article>

    </div>
  </div>
</div>
